A simple Sieve of Eratosthenes works well here. Start by assuming every number from 2 to `n` is prime. When a prime `p` is found, mark its multiples as composite. The marking can begin at `p * p` because smaller multiples were already handled by smaller primes.

For example, with `n = 10`, the primes counted are `2, 3, 5, 7`, so the answer is `4`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int primeUptoN(int n) {
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<bool> isPrime(n + 1, true);
        isPrime[0] = false;
        isPrime[1] = false;

        for (int p = 2; p * p <= n; ++p) {
            if (!isPrime[p]) {
                continue;
            }

            for (int multiple = p * p; multiple <= n; multiple += p) {
                isPrime[multiple] = false;
            }
        }

        int count = 0;
        for (int value = 2; value <= n; ++value) {
            if (isPrime[value]) {
                ++count;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log log n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)`.

Only odd numbers need to be stored after handling `2`. The sieve marks odd multiples of each odd prime. This cuts the memory roughly in half and avoids work on even values.

A small edge case is `n < 2`, where there are no primes. For `n = 3`, the array keeps `3` marked as prime and the returned count is `2`.

```python
class Solution:
    def primeUptoN(self, n):
        if n < 2:
            return 0
        if n == 2:
            return 1

        size = n // 2 + 1
        is_prime_odd = [True] * size
        is_prime_odd[0] = False

        limit = int(n ** 0.5)
        for prime in range(3, limit + 1, 2):
            if not is_prime_odd[prime // 2]:
                continue

            first_multiple = prime * prime
            for multiple in range(first_multiple, n + 1, 2 * prime):
                is_prime_odd[multiple // 2] = False

        return 1 + sum(is_prime_odd[1:n // 2 + 1])
```

The `1 +` counts the prime number `2`. Every remaining position represents one odd number.

Time complexity: `O(n log log n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)`.

This version checks every number separately. For a candidate `x`, it only tries divisors up to `sqrt(x)`. If none divide it, the number is prime.

The cases `0`, `1`, and negative values return zero immediately. For `n = 10`, the checks find four primes: `2`, `3`, `5`, and `7`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int primeUptoN(int n) {
        int count = 0;

        for (int value = 2; value <= n; ++value) {
            if (isPrime(value)) {
                ++count;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }

    private boolean isPrime(int value) {
        if (value < 2) {
            return false;
        }
        if (value == 2) {
            return true;
        }
        if (value % 2 == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int divisor = 3; (long) divisor * divisor <= value; divisor += 2) {
            if (value % divisor == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

The `long` multiplication in the loop condition avoids overflow when the input is large.

Time complexity: `O(n sqrt(n))` in the worst case.

Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This uses a linear sieve. Each composite number is marked once, using its smallest prime factor. It is a little more code than the usual sieve, but it gives a clean `O(n)` generation step.

For `n = 1`, the loop never adds a prime and the answer stays zero. The input array is not changed because this method creates its own lists.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int primeUptoN(int n) {
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<int> primes;
        vector<bool> composite(n + 1, false);

        for (int value = 2; value <= n; ++value) {
            if (!composite[value]) {
                primes.push_back(value);
            }

            for (int prime : primes) {
                long long product = 1LL * value * prime;
                if (product > n) {
                    break;
                }

                composite[static_cast<int>(product)] = true;
                if (value % prime == 0) {
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        return static_cast<int>(primes.size());
    }
};
```

The break after `value % prime == 0` is what prevents a composite from being processed more than once.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)`.

Instead of making a full sieve table, this keeps the primes found so far. A new number is tested only against those earlier primes, and the test stops when the prime is larger than the candidate's square root.

This is useful when keeping the code small matters more than the speed of a full sieve. The dry run for `n = 5` is: add `2`, add `3`, reject `4` because of `2`, then add `5`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int primeUptoN(int n) {
        if (n < 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int candidate = 2; candidate <= n; ++candidate) {
            if (isPrime(candidate, primes)) {
                primes.add(candidate);
            }
        }

        return primes.size();
    }

    private boolean isPrime(int candidate, List<Integer> primes) {
        for (int prime : primes) {
            if ((long) prime * prime > candidate) {
                break;
            }
            if (candidate % prime == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

The prime list also acts as the result storage, so no separate count table is needed. Values below `2` are handled before the list is built.

Time complexity: about `O(n sqrt(n))` in the worst case.

Space complexity: `O(pi(n))`, where `pi(n)` is the number of primes up to `n`.

